  "strategy": "Approach and rationale\nThe current approach uses random initializations and local optimization with SLSQP on a fixed 280-point discretization. This is susceptible to getting stuck in local optima. The new approach avoids this by using a combination of guided search strategies: a multi-stage optimization process where we first optimize local neighborhoods using gradient-based methods, then use a genetic algorithm (GA) for global exploration. The GA explores a larger portion of the search space using crossover and mutation operators, escape sequences, and guided mutations. This addresses the local optimum avoidance bottleneck by leveraging both local refinement and global exploration.\n\nKey differentials from standard methods:\n1. Two-phase optimization: \n   - Local phase: Optimizes near promising regions using gradient-based methods\n   - Global phase: Uses GA operators with repair mechanisms and adaptive restarts\n2. Repair mechanism:\n   - Ensures feasibility via projection and constraint enforcement\n3. Hybrid crossover:\n   - Uses blend crossover for real-valued vectors\n4. Specialized mutation:\n   - Explores promising directions based on parent knowledge\n\nThis structured approach ensures we escape local optima while maintaining the original objective's accuracy.\n\nMathematical specification\nRepresentation: Piecewise-constant function over [0,2] discretized into 280 points. Each candidate is represented by a vector h \u2208 \u211d^{280} satisfying 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i, and \u222b h(x) dx = 1 (equivalent to \u2211h[i] = 140.0).\n\nObjective: Minimize C\u2085 = max_{k} \u222b h(x)\u00b7(1-h(x+k)) dx, computed via:\n  corr = np.correlate(np.clip(h, 0, 1), 1-np.clip(h, 0, 1), mode='full')\n  C\u2085 = dx * np.max(|corr|)\n\nConstraints:\n  Lower bound: np.clip(h, 0, 1) in each entry\n  Sum constraint: \u2211h[i] = 140.0\n\nThe objective is nonconvex and separable but exhibits large-scale structure.\n\nImplementation procedure\n\n1. Strategy initialization\n   - Set DX = 2.0/280, N_POINTS = 280, TARGET_SUM = 140.0\n   - Load parent solution if available (initial_h_values)\n   - Initialize exploration points:\n     * Parent solution (if available)\n     * Small Gaussian perturbations of parent\n     * Random solutions with same sum\n\n2. Local optimization phase\n   - Use SLSQP with Hessian approximation to optimize each exploration point for 50 function calls\n   - Track the best candidate from this phase (lowest C\u2085)\n\n3. Global optimization phase\n   - Initialize GA population with candidates from:\n     + Local optimization best\n     + Boundary-based perturbations (near 0.0 and 1.0)\n     + Random restarts with normalization\n\n   - GA operators:\n     * Blend crossover (with probability 0.9)\n     * Gaussian mutation (with probability 0.1)\n     * Gaussian repair: Project infeasible vectors to [0,1]^{280} and normalize sum\n     * Clipped mutation: Only mutate within 0\u00b10.1\n     * Hybrid crossover: Mix parents and prefer larger segments\n\n   - GA parameters:\n     + Population size: 50\n     + Generations: 180 (limited to 100 seconds)\n     + Tournament selection with elitism\n     * Function evaluation limit: 5000\n\n4. Candidate processing:\n   - All candidates undergo feasibility repair:\n     Let h_safe = numpy.clip(h, 0, 1)\n     Sum_safe = sum(h_safe)\n     If Sum_safe \u2248 0: h_safe = np.random.rand(N_POINTS) * 0.1\n     Else: h_safe = (h_safe / Sum_safe) * TARGET_SUM\n     h_safe = np.clip(h_safe, 0, 1)\n\n   - Repair preserves all other constraints.\n\nOptimization hyperparameters:\n- Local phase iterations: 50\n- GA population: 50\n- GA generations: 180\n- Repair attempts: 3 per operator\n- Function evaluation limit: 5000\n\nCompute budget and robustness\n\nEstimate function evaluations:\n- Local: 80 candidates \u00d7 50 calls = 4000 evaluations\n- Global: 5000 evaluations budgeted\n- Total: 9000 evaluations\n\nTime estimates (CPUs=1):\n- Objective: Mode='full' correlation takes O(280*log(280)) time, approx 0.0002s per eval\n- Repair: O(280) per candidate\n- Total runtime: ~2 seconds for local + ~2 seconds for global\n\nTermination conditions:\n- GA generations complete or time exhausted\n- Local optimization finds non-improving candidates\n\nValidation and return contract\n1. Feasibility checks:\n   - Sum(h)\u2248140.0 (tolerance 1e-4)\n   - All values in [0.0,1.0]\n   - No NaN/inf\n\n2. Objective evaluation:\n   - Compute with projection, repair, and scaling\n   - Ignore candidates with inf/NaN values\n\n3. Return format:\n   - numpy array of 280 doubles satisfying [0,1] and \u2211=140.0\n   - Minimal C\u2085 possible\n\nSpecial handling for large C\u2085:\n   - Return parent candidate if exceeding 0.382 (unless initial solution is used)\n\nThese steps ensure the algorithm explores the solution space effectively while maintaining feasibility and adhering to the problem constraints.",
